nsitive substring. So we must include the sentence with lowercase \"a\"? If our sentence starts \"A release that changes...\" then the needle \"a release that changes...\" is a substring of it? \"A release...\" vs \"a release...\" \u2014 case differs at first char only. `\" \".join(...)` search is case-sensitive. So \"A release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\" does NOT contain \"a release ...\". Hmm. But the `\\n`-joined variant... also case-sensitive.\n\nSo write it such that the lowercase \"a\" appears: e.g. \"...; a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\" That gives exact match.\n\nCareful about the wrap: carried_verbatim handles wraps.\n\nNow careful: the test's insert uses `t.replace(\"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0\", ...)` \u2014 that string must exist in our CONTRIBUTING (it does, if we keep the cadence sentence). And it must be in the Releases section. Yes.\n\nBut wait: our amendment placement: after \"; \" we get \"...starts with 0; a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence, quarterly after 1.0.\" Hmm that reads awkwardly. Let me restructure the Releases section so the insertion point works but the text reads fine:\n\nActual CONTRIBUTING must contain the amendment. Let me write:\n\n```\n## Releases\n\nAt most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a\ngit tag, and is cited by its commit.\n\nA release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\n```\n\nThen the plant inserts after \"starts with 0\" \u2192 \"... starts with 0; a release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence, quarterly after 1.0. ...\" \u2014 the amendment appears twice; carried_verbatim fine.\n\nNow the sentence-level comparison (d) in measure_stale_stamp: base_units of CONTRIBUTING vs now_units masked. UNSTABLE_PREFIXES = (\"At most one release a month\", \"Versions follow Semantic Versioning\", \"Every release is a git tag\"). Our changes to the Releases section:\n\nBase lines:\n```\n## Releases\n(blank)\nAt most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a\ngit tag, and is cited by its commit.\n```\n\nsentences() logic: for a line starting with \"#\" \u2192 flush, append the line itself. Then \"At most one release a month ... Versions follow\" \u2014 buf = that whole line. Next line \"Semantic Versioning 2.0.0; ... Every release is a\" \u2014 buf += \" \" + s (since buf doesn't end with -). Next line \"git tag, and is cited by its commit.\" \u2192 appended.\n\nSo base unit = \"At most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow Semantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a git tag, and is cited by its commit.\"\n\nWait, does the base have a blank line after \"## Releases\"? Let me re-read:\n\n```\n## Releases\n\nAt most one release a month while the version starts with 0, quarterly after 1.0. Versions follow\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0; `SPEC.md` section 8 says what counts as a major change. Every release is a\ngit tag, and is cited by its commit.\n```\n\nYes blank line. So unit is the joined paragraph.\n\nIn (d): `any(s.startswith(u) for u in UNSTABLE_PREFIXES)` \u2014 our base unit starts with \"At most one release a month\" \u2192 unstable branch: head = first 8 words = \"At most one release a month while the version\" \u2192 must appear in some new unit (masked). \n\nHmm! \"while the version starts with 0\" \u2014 the head 8 words: \"At most one release a month while the version\". If I keep that phrase, fine.\n\nBut also: for stable base units the masked comparison must hold. If I split the Releases paragraph into two paragraphs, the base unit (unstable, handled by head check) is fine. But what about... the sentence \"Every release is a git tag, and is cited by its commit.\" is part of that unstable unit, so covered by the head check.\n\nCareful: does the paragraph break create issues elsewhere? The units after the Releases paragraph: next paragraph \"## Before you open a pull request\" heading etc.\n\nHmm, but wait: if I insert a new paragraph \"A release that changes no requirement does not count toward the monthly cadence.\", that's a new unit \u2014 not a base unit, so no problem.\n\nHowever: does the base unit's head appear in the new units? New units will include the \"At most one release...\" paragraph (unchanged) \u2192 head\n\n\"At most one release a month while the version\" in masked new unit \u2713.\n\nHmm but actually careful: masked_now = {_body_without_versions(s) for s in now_units} and then `any(head in _body_without_versions(n) for n in now_units)`. head has no version tokens. \u2713\n\nNow, another consideration: UNSTABLE_PREFIXES for \"Versions follow Semantic Versioning\" and \"Every release is a git tag\" are for the case where those are separate paragraphs. Not needed.\n\nNow careful with the CONTRIBUTING fact paragraph rewrite and (d) for base units: base units include:\n1. \"A behavioral change to the checker, the fixtures or the schema is itself a job: the bar is frozen before the change, a reader who could not see the maker's answer reads it, and the release carrying it ships a record.\" \u2014 wait, sentences() joins the lines of the paragraph, so this whole paragraph is one unit including the \"Version 0.9.8 shipped ...\" sentence, and that unit is a fact unit (carries_version_fact true) \u2192 exempted.\n\nHmm, so the fact unit includes the first sentences. Then rewriting the fact sentence keeps this unit a fact unit; but base unit is exempt by identity. But then - careful - the new unit must still be a fact-carrying unit for (b). Yes.\n\nBut hold on: there's a risk. The base unit includes EVERYTHING in that paragraph. If our new paragraph structure differs... it's exempt anyway.\n\n2. Other base units must survive masked. We change:\n- the fact paragraph (exempt).\n- the Releases paragraph (unstable).\n- possibly the \"Version 0.9.8 shipped...\" is in the same paragraph as \"A behavioral change...\"? Let me look at base:\n\n```\nA behavioral change without a failing record or counterexample is closed, not argued. Records made to\ntest this specification itself do not count as real use.\n\nA behavioral change to the checker, the fixtures or the schema is itself a job: the bar is frozen before the\nchange, a reader who could not see the maker's answer reads it, and the release carrying it ships a record.\nVersion 0.9.8 shipped without one \u2014 `record/RECORD-GAP-v0.9.8.md` says why \u2014 and `count.py` now refuses a\nrelease that neither carries a record nor declares its absence.\n```\n\nYes: second paragraph ends with the fact. So the whole second paragraph is one fact unit. Exempt.\n\nGood: only that paragraph changes plus the Releases section.\n\nNow the \"honest rewrite\" clears test: `self.assertEqual(measure_stale_stamp(cleared), [])`. cleared is subject text with fact paragraph passed through honest_rewrite; since our text has no \"Version 0.9.8 shipped without one\", unchanged \u2192 same as subject \u2192 measure_stale_stamp(subject) must be [] (already required by test_07's opening assertion). \u2713\n\nNow test_02's bite 2: `stale_root = self._edit(\"README.md\", lambda t: t + f\"\\n\\nEarlier: {stale}.\\n\")` where stale = first of 0.9.1..0.9.39 != 0.9.9 \u2192 0.9.1. Then assert_bites(measure_count_except_record, stale_root, f\"version {stale}\") \u2192 count.py reports \"version 0.9.1 in README.md disagrees with SPEC.md 0.9.9\" \u2192 measure_count_except_record bad includes f\"count.py: {p}\" containing \"version 0.9.1\" \u2